<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/83481302-01ca-452f-82a5-b6dc3f338b97> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#ArchitecturalDecision> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/83481302-01ca-452f-82a5-b6dc3f338b97> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "A collective RBox failure omits the whole RBox" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/83481302-01ca-452f-82a5-b6dc3f338b97> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/83481302-01ca-452f-82a5-b6dc3f338b97> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "When the RBox fails a collective gate — property-chain regularity being the case in point — the ENTIRE RBox goes into coverage.omitted, the run is inconclusive, and the witness is reported as diagnostics for a human to act on. HOWL does not classify a projected RBox.\n\nWhy not delete the witness and carry on: that needs a REMOVAL SET, and a witness is not one. A witness may contain several chains and simple inclusions, deleting any of several alternatives can restore regularity, and each choice yields a different residual theory. Worse, the choice is NOT LOCAL. Concretely, given `a ∘ b ⊑ c`, `c ⊑ a`, `p ∘ c ⊑ t`, `range(t) = C`, `range(a) = C`: the axiom `p ∘ c ⊑ t` initially passes the range gate because `c ⊑ a` makes C an inherited range of c. Delete `c ⊑ a` as part of the regularity witness and the residual theory keeps `p ∘ c ⊑ t` and `range(t) = C` while losing the range on c — so range elimination runs OUTSIDE ITS OWN PRECONDITION, and a run reported complete over the residual theory may not be.\n\nRepairing that properly would need a deletion algorithm with tie-breaking, atomic removal of desugared axioms with their origin, a combination rule for simultaneous violations, and gate re-runs to stability. Every one of those is a choice two implementations would make differently.\n\nSo HOWL does not choose. A deterministic, reproducible refusal is worth more here than a larger set of results nobody can reproduce. TBox-only omissions are unaffected — this rule is about the RBox specifically, whose axioms interact through two gates at once.\n\nThe witness itself still needs a canonical policy, since non-regularity is a property of an axiom SET and the refs are hashed into verdict identity: it is read off the strict-order CONSTRAINT graph (not the role-inclusion graph), with each axiom contributing one dominance-selected constraint set, and witnesses that are set-valued and choice-free per failure kind.\n\nEvidence: SPEC.md §5.2 (the collective-failure paragraph and its counterexample, witness policy), §6.2 (Omission), §10 test 8." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/83481302-01ca-452f-82a5-b6dc3f338b97> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/83481302-01ca-452f-82a5-b6dc3f338b97> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTimestamp> "2026-08-17T22:28:51.939816Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
