<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/b36adc54-6a36-49c7-bb41-a4744cb6caab> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#isMotivatedBy> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/65db656d-afe2-49bc-80aa-0d3677bca2e9> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/b36adc54-6a36-49c7-bb41-a4744cb6caab> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#supersedes> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/ff60d323-bdc6-4e9a-ae99-acb6e214e62f> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/d8de2a24-87ef-4e03-a675-60d3050c604d> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#ArchitecturalDecision> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/d8de2a24-87ef-4e03-a675-60d3050c604d> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "The index sort is an in-place bottom-up merge over two buffers" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/d8de2a24-87ef-4e03-a675-60d3050c604d> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/d8de2a24-87ef-4e03-a675-60d3050c604d> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "canon.slop sorts an index permutation with a width-doubling bottom-up merge over two preallocated buffers, merged into alternately with `list-set`, rather than the recursive top-down merge that split and allocated a fresh list per level.\n\nWHY NOW, AND WHY IT IS NOT A REVERSAL. The top-down form was not a misjudgement; it was the only shape expressible. SLOP lists were append-only — `list-new`, `list-push`, `list-get`, `list-len` — so no in-place permutation could be written at all, and every sort had to rebuild. slop 0.2.3 shipped `list-set` (slop-lang/slop#124, filed off HOWL's own issue #121), which removed that limit. The recorded Alternative rejecting insertion sort turns on the exact phrase \"with no list-set in the language\", and the recorded Consequence already flagged the O(n log n) allocation as \"worth revisiting\". This is that revisit, triggered by the language gaining the operation rather than by profiling — no GO-scale measurement was taken.\n\nTHE ALLOCATION ARGUMENT IS ABOUT THE ARENA, NOT ABOUT SPEED. Under -DSLOP_ARENA_NO_CAP nothing is ever freed, so per-level temporaries are not transient scratch that the allocator reclaims — they are RETAINED for the life of the run. The recursive form retains ~2n·log2(n) index slots; this form retains 2n. At GO's 73,634 subsumptions that is ~2.5M slots against ~147K. Comparison count is unchanged at O(n log n); only allocation improves, from O(n log n) to O(n).\n\nIT IS ALSO LESS CODE. `take-range`, `merge-idx` and `sort-idx` collapse into `merge-run` plus `sort-range` — about 51 body lines down to 35 — and the recursion disappears. The simplification is what justifies the change independently of the allocation win; had the in-place form been more intricate, waiting for profiling would have been right.\n\nTWO MECHANICS WERE PROBED, NOT ASSUMED, because upstream's list-set tests cover neither: (1) `list-set` writes through a by-value `(List Int)` parameter, because a list compiles to a value struct {len, cap, data} and the write goes through `data` without reallocating — this works ONLY because the write is length-preserving, and the same shape with `list-push` would grow the copy and discard it on return; (2) assigning one list struct to another swaps which buffer each name denotes, which is what makes the per-pass role swap legal. A probe returning 9902 confirmed both.\n\nVERIFICATION IS EMPIRICAL BY NECESSITY, NOT BY CHOICE. slop#124 explicitly WITHHOLDS the provenance axioms for any body that calls `list-set`, so the multiset claim is out of the WP checker's reach by construction. The obligation is stated in-source and discharged by test: permutation, order, and stability. Mutation-tested — flipping the tie rule from `<=` to `<` reddens the stability test alone; disabling the left-tail copy reddens all three.\n\nSTABILITY GAINED A TEST IT DID NOT HAVE. It is invisible through `sort-iris`, because `iri-cmp` is a total order and tied elements are byte-identical, so any permutation of them looks correct. It is observable only through the raw `sort-range` entry point with a comparator that ties elements the test can still tell apart. This matters because comparators here do legitimately tie distinct values — `missing-declaration-cmp` ties on equal referring-lists — and if ties resolved by run boundaries their order would be a function of list LENGTH rather than content." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/d8de2a24-87ef-4e03-a675-60d3050c604d> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/d8de2a24-87ef-4e03-a675-60d3050c604d> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTimestamp> "2026-09-12T05:01:39.400742Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
