<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/d8de2a24-87ef-4e03-a675-60d3050c604d> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#weighs> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/Alternative/ee78df44-5919-46d5-8785-4add9ce54669> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/f15852f8-c7e1-4d93-a347-d915de5e041e> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#ArchitecturalDecision> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/f15852f8-c7e1-4d93-a347-d915de5e041e> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Δ commits at the barrier, so a round never writes the store" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/f15852f8-c7e1-4d93-a347-d915de5e041e> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/f15852f8-c7e1-4d93-a347-d915de5e041e> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "The saturation driver commits Δₙ into the context store at the END of round n−1. During round n the store therefore IS the frozen snapshot Sₙ = Sₙ₋₁ ∪ Δₙ — with no snapshot copy taken — and it is frozen because nothing writes it. Δₙ travels separately in `Saturation.queues`, so rules still know which facts are new. A round is `round-join` (strictly read-only over `sat`, emitting into a driver-owned RoundDelta) followed by `round-commit` (the barrier, and the only mutation site).\n\nWHY THIS IS FORCED RATHER THAN CHOSEN. SPEC.md §6.4 requires conclusions to be HELD for Δₙ₊₁ and never folded into Sₙ as they are produced. In SLOP that is not merely easy to get wrong — it is the DEFAULT failure. `(Set T)` compiles to a `slop_map*` pointer, so a Context read out of the registry shares its subsumer set with the stored one; the obvious implementation that adds conclusions to `subsumers` as they are derived silently ALIASES the frozen snapshot with the live store, and a fact derived early in round n becomes visible to rules still running in round n. Determinism is lost with no symptom.\n\nTwo independent runtime hazards forbid mutate-as-you-go anyway: `slop_map_put` rehashes at 75% load while `for-each` re-reads `cap` each iteration (mutating a set under iteration silently skips or revisits elements), and `slop_map_remove` clears `occupied` with no tombstone in a linear-probe table whose `get` stops at the first empty slot (a removal can strand later chain members). Hence `active` is REBUILT each barrier, never pruned.\n\nWHAT MAKES THE INVARIANT ENFORCED RATHER THAN DOCUMENTED. It reduces to one reviewable property: `round-join` and everything it calls contain no `set-put`/`map-put`/`list-push` targeting anything reachable from `sat`. And it is enforced mechanically — the read-only accessors (`context-of`, `queue-of`, `store-has-sub`, `store-has-succ`) take NO Arena parameter, while `set-put`/`map-put`/`set-elements` all emit the bare C identifier `arena`. A store write in the join phase is a COMPILE ERROR, not a review finding.\n\nNOVELTY (`\\ Sₙ`) HAS ONE SITE, `admit`, called per emitted message during the join. Not inside the rules: CR4 runs in Y and emits to X, so a rule cannot see its conclusion's destination, and a store read there would poison its faithfulness contracts. Not at commit either: testing there still terminates, but every context that derived nothing new would land in the frontier.\n\nEDGES ARE TESTED AS LOGICAL EDGES, as §6.4 requires. Both stored halves normalize back to the same triple — a `derived-pred (r,x)` addressed to y IS the edge (x,r,y) — so whichever half the driver sees reaches one test on the successor side, and admission writes BOTH halves or neither. That is sound because commit never writes one half without the other, making `succ(X,r,Y) ∈ store ⟺ pred(Y,r,X) ∈ store` a store invariant maintained by construction.\n\nINITIALIZATION IS THE FIRST BARRIER. §6.3 step 4's \"empty `subsumers`\" describes S₋₁; the state the driver first sees is S₀ = Δ₀, so the seeds must be committed to the store as well as queued. Reading it as queue-only is silent and total: CR2 can never find the other conjunct of a `sub-and`, CR4 can never satisfy B ∈ S(Y) against a seed, and the §3.1 litmus — which joins the arriving edge against the SEED `Person ∈ S(Person)` — fails, reverting the engine to a materializer.\n\nVerified by mutation: skipping the novelty test makes the cyclic-hierarchy fixture hit the round cap instead of fixpoint; omitting the seed commit makes the litmus fail. Implemented in src/saturate.slop; 17/17 tests, 23 verified contracts." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/f15852f8-c7e1-4d93-a347-d915de5e041e> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/f15852f8-c7e1-4d93-a347-d915de5e041e> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTimestamp> "2026-08-20T04:37:40.558096Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
