<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/007e982c-4936-4607-8bda-33451359f836> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#Constraint> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/007e982c-4936-4607-8bda-33451359f836> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Reports are input-determined" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/007e982c-4936-4607-8bda-33451359f836> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#constrains> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/85f90bba-3140-46ac-aa9f-47d76dbd2064> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/007e982c-4936-4607-8bda-33451359f836> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/007e982c-4936-4607-8bda-33451359f836> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "Byte-identical canonicalized input plus the same budget MUST produce an identical canonical report. No wall clock, no randomness, no network inside a reasoning call.\n\nThis is a hard external contract, not an internal preference: the consumer content-hashes the report into a verdict identity, stores verdicts as immutable append-only records, and re-confirms them later. A report that varies run-over-run does not merely look untidy — it breaks verdict identity and every downstream guarantee built on it.\n\nWhere the risk actually is: NOT in the complete case. EL saturation is confluent, so a run reaching fixpoint derives the same conclusion set regardless of processing order — scheduling cannot change a completed answer. The entire exposure is in NON-complete runs. A resource-limit run stops at whatever partial state the scheduler produced, and with racing workers that state is a function of thread timing: two identical runs at the same budget could report different unsatisfiable classes, or one `fixpoint` and the other `resource-limit`. Under the verdict discipline the second is a DIFFERENT VERDICT, so this is a correctness bug, not a performance wobble.\n\nWhat it forces, concretely:\n- `iteration` is a deterministic global round with a barrier, never a per-worker message count, so max-iterations cuts at the same point on every host at any worker count.\n- Worker count must not appear in the engine's config hash — that would make verdicts non-reproducible across deployments and defeat re-validation.\n- Cancellation yields no report at all: a cancelled partial state cannot be made reproducible, so it must be UNREPRESENTABLE as a result rather than merely discouraged. Nothing from a cancelled run may be serialized or hashed.\n- HOWL never consults a clock; wall-clock timeouts belong to the host, so a timeout can only produce an inconclusive verdict and can never flip a pass to a fail.\n- Every result field is canonically ordered, and the budget's semantics are pinned (max-iterations = n permits n completed rounds; fixpoint on the final permitted round is success; n = 0 always yields resource-limit).\n\nAlso why AxiomRef needs an exhaustively defined canonical order and why blank nodes must be labelled by a pinned RDF canonicalization algorithm: each feeds the hash, so an unstated case is a cross-implementation hash divergence.\n\nEvidence: SPEC.md §6.8 (Determinism, binding), §6.2 (AxiomRef canonical order), §6.7 (canonical ordering of result fields)." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/007e982c-4936-4607-8bda-33451359f836> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/007e982c-4936-4607-8bda-33451359f836> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTimestamp> "2026-08-17T22:26:21.223545Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/007e982c-4936-4607-8bda-33451359f836> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTitle> "Reports are input-determined" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/11c160af-1cdd-477f-9129-c3acd1b88622> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#Constraint> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/11c160af-1cdd-477f-9129-c3acd1b88622> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Classification within 5x ELK under a pinned protocol" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/11c160af-1cdd-477f-9129-c3acd1b88622> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/11c160af-1cdd-477f-9129-c3acd1b88622> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "M1 acceptance requires HOWL's classification time to be within 5x ELK's on the same corpus and machine, measured under a protocol pinned in the repo. 5x is a starting line, not a target: loose enough to be achievable for a first implementation, tight enough that a wrong data structure fails it. Bounds the Requirement \"Coherence validation inside an authoring/CI loop\".\n\nThe protocol exists because the phrase it replaced — \"a small multiple of ELK\" — named no corpus version, no hardware, no worker count and no measurement boundary, so it could not be passed or failed:\n- Corpus: GO and GALEN at pinned release versions, recorded together with their v0 projections and hashes, plus SNOMED CT if a licence is available. \"GALEN\" names several ontologies of wildly differing difficulty, so the variant is part of the pin.\n- Comparison scope: benchmark against ELK ONLY over the capability-probed common subset. Range-bearing entries are timed separately against a confirmed-complete oracle — comparing HOWL doing the work against ELK skipping it measures nothing.\n- Measurement: wall-clock of classification only, excluding parse and serialization, measured separately from end-to-end; median of 5 runs after 1 warm-up, single machine, worker count fixed at 4 and reported alongside.\n- ELK's own numbers are regenerated on the same machine, never quoted from the literature.\n\nRelated hazard this closes: every benchmark input must produce empty `coverage.omitted`. A timing comparison over an ontology HOWL only partly processed makes HOWL look fast precisely to the extent that it skipped work, and the failure does not announce itself.\n\nEvidence: SPEC.md §12 M1 acceptance (c) and its benchmark-protocol block, §10 item 2 (corpus projection), §2.1 (the underlying time requirement)." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/11c160af-1cdd-477f-9129-c3acd1b88622> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/11c160af-1cdd-477f-9129-c3acd1b88622> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTimestamp> "2026-08-17T22:27:22.00336Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/11c160af-1cdd-477f-9129-c3acd1b88622> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTitle> "Classification within 5x ELK under a pinned protocol" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/456acb54-a6ff-4970-a062-863892f2825f> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#Constraint> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/456acb54-a6ff-4970-a062-863892f2825f> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "A port amendment gates its capability" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/456acb54-a6ff-4970-a062-863892f2825f> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/456acb54-a6ff-4970-a062-863892f2825f> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "Until a required amendment to the frozen `TBoxReasoner` port lands, the capability it unblocks is NOT DELIVERED — it is not approximated, and the adapter never fabricates a value the interface cannot carry. `TBoxInput` is `{ triples: Vec<Triple> }`; that is the whole of it.\n\nThe four amendments and what their absence costs:\n- A1 — per-document triple buffers or a source IRI per triple. Without it, flattening loses document identity, so equivalent axioms from different mapping documents are indistinguishable and blank nodes from different documents can merge. Partial workaround: AxiomRef.source is Optional and the CLI populates it, so source-sensitive repair works from the CLI and degrades to canonical-text identity through the port — a real capability difference between the two surfaces.\n- A2 — `imports_resolved` closure attestation. Without it, a root document with a dangling import yields empty omissions, a fixpoint, and a FALSE COHERENT.\n- A3 — a baseline channel AND `subsumption_delta: Option<Vec<_>>`. The current unconditional Vec has no value for \"no delta was computed\", so an empty vector claims \"no new subsumptions\" — a wrong answer rather than a missing one. Either A3 lands with the Option or baseline-diff leaves scope. Gates the Requirement \"New subsumptions against a committed baseline\".\n- A4 — probe-set entailment readback. Without it the consumer's agreement stage fails closed for any non-stub engine, so landing HOWL makes the pipeline LESS usable than the stub it replaces.\n\nWhy this is a constraint rather than a scheduling note: every tempting workaround is worse than the gap. Hidden state to fake a baseline breaks determinism and fingerprinting; returning the full candidate hierarchy mislabels every subsumption as new; returning an empty delta is a silent wrong answer. This is the same discipline the verdict rule applies to reasoning results, applied to the adapter.\n\nSequencing: A1–A2 gate M2's correctness claims, A3–A4 gate M2's feature claims, so the amendments are their own milestone (M2a) ahead of the adapter.\n\nEvidence: SPEC.md §8.5 (Required port amendments), §8.4, §12 M2a/M2b." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/456acb54-a6ff-4970-a062-863892f2825f> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/456acb54-a6ff-4970-a062-863892f2825f> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTimestamp> "2026-08-17T22:27:14.456081Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/456acb54-a6ff-4970-a062-863892f2825f> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTitle> "A port amendment gates its capability" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/65db656d-afe2-49bc-80aa-0d3677bca2e9> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#Constraint> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/65db656d-afe2-49bc-80aa-0d3677bca2e9> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Only a complete run may report coherent" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/65db656d-afe2-49bc-80aa-0d3677bca2e9> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#constrains> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/00683550-a89a-4865-8633-222f7600d5e6> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/65db656d-afe2-49bc-80aa-0d3677bca2e9> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#constrains> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/3eacfa8e-800e-452f-9547-e4ee85c2f45d> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/65db656d-afe2-49bc-80aa-0d3677bca2e9> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/65db656d-afe2-49bc-80aa-0d3677bca2e9> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "A run is COMPLETE exactly when `coverage.omitted` is empty AND `termination` is `fixpoint`. That conjunction — not a status tag — is what licenses a coherent verdict, a complete hierarchy, or a minimal repair.\n\nStatus is three INDEPENDENT axes, never one union:\n- `Coverage` — which axioms were processed. Empty `omitted` means full coverage; any Omission (out-of-profile, unresolved-import, missing-declaration) means the run saw less than the intended theory.\n- `Termination` — `fixpoint` or `resource-limit(n)`. Cancellation is NOT here: it yields `Fault::cancelled` with no Outcome, because a cancelled partial state is scheduler-dependent, cannot be made reproducible, and must never be serialized or hashed.\n- `Findings` — what was derived. Always present, always meaningful.\n\nThey are independent because a run can be both out-of-profile and resource-limited at once. The consumer's port models them as separate fields (`out_of_profile_axioms` alongside `fixpoint_reached`), and the mapping from a union could not be written without losing information — the consumer's data model was the check on ours.\n\nTHE VERDICT RULE, which is three-way and ASYMMETRIC:\n\n| Findings | Coverage + termination | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| incoherent | anything | INCOHERENT — definitive, even under partial coverage |\n| not incoherent | omitted non-empty, or termination ≠ fixpoint | INCONCLUSIVE |\n| not incoherent | omitted empty AND fixpoint | COHERENT |\n\nThe asymmetry between rows 1 and 2 is not a compromise; it falls out of MONOTONICITY. Every rule is faithful, so anything in findings is sound whatever the coverage; and restoring an omitted axiom can only ADD conclusions, never retract one, so a ⊥ derived from a subset of the ontology is a ⊥ in the whole ontology. A finding is definitive; the ABSENCE of a finding is what carries no information. An earlier draft sent every out-of-profile run to inconclusive, which discarded sound results.\n\nWhy the safety direction matters: HOWL's flagship service is `validate` as a CI gate. Several paths reach a non-fixpoint state, and a validator that silently returns \"coherent\" when it could not tell is worse than no validator, because CI trains users to trust it. Surfaces as exit code 2 (inconclusive), distinct from 0 (coherent) and 1 (incoherent); `--strict` controls only whether HOWL refuses to run versus returns a marked-partial result, and NO flag can promote an incomplete run to a verdict. The same monotonicity is why a partial run may never report a COMPLETE hierarchy or a MINIMAL repair — those are claims about absence, and a hitting set over an incomplete justification set is not minimal.\n\nThe port's fault precedence and the CLI's exit codes are both this table in their own dialects; neither may restate it differently.\n\nEvidence: SPEC.md §6.2 (Outcome, the verdict rule and its diagram), §6.7, §8.4 (fault precedence), §9 (exit codes), §10 item 4." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/65db656d-afe2-49bc-80aa-0d3677bca2e9> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/65db656d-afe2-49bc-80aa-0d3677bca2e9> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasRationale> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/Rationale/727523b8-7bf5-4699-a70a-79e144305c98> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/65db656d-afe2-49bc-80aa-0d3677bca2e9> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTimestamp> "2026-08-17T22:56:58.129987Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/65db656d-afe2-49bc-80aa-0d3677bca2e9> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTitle> "Only a complete run may report coherent" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
