<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/0165b8ad-6735-4c1b-b8dc-970c9bc1b612> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#isSupersededBy> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/977a6eb2-028a-405d-b048-63ae24fb3ad2> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/0165b8ad-6735-4c1b-b8dc-970c9bc1b612> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#learnedFrom> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/f15852f8-c7e1-4d93-a347-d915de5e041e> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/277488f7-ebb2-4785-8618-26a60f727a79> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#Lesson> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/277488f7-ebb2-4785-8618-26a60f727a79> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "SLOP list-push invents an arena member when none is in scope" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/277488f7-ebb2-4785-8618-26a60f727a79> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/277488f7-ebb2-4785-8618-26a60f727a79> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "`(list-push (. s field) v)` inside a function with NO `arena` binding in scope lowers to `slop_arena_alloc(s.arena, …)` — the transpiler synthesizes an `arena` member on the list's base type rather than reporting that it has no arena to allocate from. The failure surfaces in `cc` as \"no member named 'arena' in 'struct X'\", not as a checker error.\n\nWith an arena in scope the same expression lowers correctly, which is why `saturate.slop`'s `seed-node` has always compiled while an otherwise identical helper does not.\n\nVERIFIED BY ISOLATION, and the first diagnosis was wrong. The initial reading was \"list-push into a record FIELD does not compile, because the arena is derived from the base expression\" — and a workaround was written into HOWL's source on that basis, copying the field to a local before pushing. A second probe that differed ONLY in taking an `arena` parameter compiled and ran correctly, which falsified it: the base being a field is irrelevant, the absent arena binding is the whole cause. The workaround and its comment were removed rather than left standing, because a comment asserting a false toolchain limitation is worse than no comment — the next reader propagates it.\n\nPRACTICAL RULE FOR HOWL: any function that mutates a container takes `Arena` explicitly and carries `@alloc arena`. That is already forced for `set-put` / `map-put` / `set-elements`, which emit the bare identifier `arena`; `list-push` belongs in the same class and its failure mode is worse because it reaches the C compiler instead of the checker.\n\nUpstream issue owed against slop: the missing diagnostic, not the lowering.\n\nGENERAL RULE: when a toolchain bug is diagnosed from one failing case, construct the minimal variant that isolates the suspected cause before writing the explanation into source. A plausible-but-wrong mechanism embedded in a comment outlives the bug." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/277488f7-ebb2-4785-8618-26a60f727a79> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/277488f7-ebb2-4785-8618-26a60f727a79> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTimestamp> "2026-09-12T18:35:48.89342Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/277488f7-ebb2-4785-8618-26a60f727a79> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTitle> "SLOP list-push invents an arena member when none is in scope" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/29204dfe-0826-425d-ae17-d4ed733edaee> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#Lesson> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/29204dfe-0826-425d-ae17-d4ed733edaee> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Route a correction to supersede, not to a fresh Lesson" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/29204dfe-0826-425d-ae17-d4ed733edaee> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/29204dfe-0826-425d-ae17-d4ed733edaee> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "When a design position changes, superseding the record that asserts the old position is the operation. Minting a NEW record about the correction feels like discharging the obligation — it is a real write, it is true, it is useful — but it retracts nothing, so the stale assertion stays `accepted` and stays in recall.\n\nMeasured on HOWL's own graph at spec approval (2026-08-17), after fourteen review rounds:\n\n| Kind | Records | Drifted |\n|---|---|---|\n| Lesson | 10 | 0 |\n| Constraint | 4 | 3 |\n| ArchitecturalDecision | 2 | 0 |\n| Requirement | 1 | 0 |\n\nEvery correction HAD been captured — as a Lesson. The Lesson layer was a complete and accurate record of the whole review; the Constraint layer was a set of snapshots frozen at the moment each was written. The three drifts:\n- A Constraint specified range support as a new completion rule CR8. The spec later removed CR8 (it would have claimed completeness from a proof not covering the implemented calculus) and captured the governing principle as a NEW Constraint, \"A gate may not be more permissive than the proof it cites\" — two days later, while the record asserting CR8 stayed accepted. The graph held the correct rule and the wrong instance simultaneously.\n- A Constraint required \"a RunStatus (complete | out-of-profile | resource-limit | cancelled)\" union. The spec replaced it with three independent axes, and the correction went into Lesson \"Reasoner status is three independent axes, not one status union\" rather than into the Constraint.\n- A Constraint's definition of `unsatisfiable` never gained the `∅ when inconsistent` case the spec added.\n\nWHY THE KIND PREDICTS DURABILITY. A Lesson is append-only by nature — \"we made this mistake, here is why\" stays true however the design moves. A Constraint or ArchitecturalDecision asserts a CURRENT position, so it perishes when the position changes. Capturing one mid-review, while the position is still being argued, is what makes it stale; the only Constraint that survived clean was the one written last.\n\nTWO PRACTICES THAT FOLLOW.\n1. Before writing a Lesson about a correction, ask which existing record the correction FALSIFIES. If one exists, supersede it — the Lesson is then optional colour, not the fix.\n2. Prefer capturing a still-moving decision as a Lesson (durable) over a Constraint (perishable) until the design settles, and mint the Constraint at approval.\n\nDETECTION. Recall truncates long descriptions at roughly 2000 characters, so comparing a spec against recall output verifies the opening of each record and nothing else — all three drifts here were in truncated tails. A real drift audit needs full descriptions via `sparql`, scoped to Constraints and ArchitecturalDecisions, since Lessons do not perish." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/29204dfe-0826-425d-ae17-d4ed733edaee> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/29204dfe-0826-425d-ae17-d4ed733edaee> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTimestamp> "2026-08-17T22:59:37.816955Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
