<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/351e4c85-8221-400b-ad97-ffc72f8d83ee> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasRationale> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/Rationale/698ea7ee-ecf1-408c-b1fb-fc9cf9e92274> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/351e4c85-8221-400b-ad97-ffc72f8d83ee> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTimestamp> "2026-08-20T04:38:34.000424Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/351e4c85-8221-400b-ad97-ffc72f8d83ee> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTitle> "The slop verify boundary: expression shape, not claim meaning" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/351e4c85-8221-400b-ad97-ffc72f8d83ee> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#isSupersededBy> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/772eff39-94f7-4692-92ea-23f39da47e50> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/351e4c85-8221-400b-ad97-ffc72f8d83ee> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#learnedFrom> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/85e6f9fb-40ea-497b-b689-3074f1a4ad52> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/351e4c85-8221-400b-ad97-ffc72f8d83ee> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#supersedes> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/bb5a926e-bd43-4a54-8e86-bac9dbfd630f> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/3e677494-0065-40c0-a54f-2b96e2cb8b80> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#Lesson> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/3e677494-0065-40c0-a54f-2b96e2cb8b80> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Semi-naive rules must fire on every premise, not just the \"new\" one" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/3e677494-0065-40c0-a54f-2b96e2cb8b80> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/3e677494-0065-40c0-a54f-2b96e2cb8b80> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "A multi-premise completion rule needs one delta handler PER PREMISE. Writing a single handler that fires when premise A arrives and scans stored premise B silently loses every conclusion whose B arrives after its A.\n\nConcretely in HOWL: CR4 joins (X,Y) ∈ R(r) with B ∈ S(Y). The draft implementation fired on a newly delivered subsumer and scanned existing predecessors. If the subsumer arrives first and the predecessor edge later, nothing re-fires CR4 and the conclusion is never derived. CR2 (two subsumers of the same context) and CR7 (two role edges) are self-joins with the same shape — a new fact must be joined against the stored set including facts of its own kind, which is where a naive \"process the delta\" loop most often loses conclusions.\n\nWhy it is nastier than an ordinary bug: which premise arrives first is a scheduling artifact, so this is simultaneously an incompleteness bug AND a determinism bug. It passes a single-threaded happy-path test, passes under one worker count, and fails under another — and the failure is a missing conclusion, which is invisible unless something checks for it.\n\nThe fix is to make the delta-trigger table part of the specified calculus rather than an implementation detail: for each rule, an explicit row naming which incoming fact kinds trigger it and which stored side each handler scans. Two associated invariants: the stored side must be the PRE-ROUND state (a round's own output joins in the next round, or deterministic round semantics break), and the test suite must construct both arrival orders for every multi-premise rule and assert identical results.\n\nGeneralizes to any semi-naive / delta-driven fixpoint, including GROWL's rule groups. Captured 2026-08-11." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/3e677494-0065-40c0-a54f-2b96e2cb8b80> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/3e677494-0065-40c0-a54f-2b96e2cb8b80> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTimestamp> "2026-08-11T15:21:21.48316Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/3e677494-0065-40c0-a54f-2b96e2cb8b80> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTitle> "Semi-naive rules must fire on every premise, not just the \"new\" one" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/48a27dcd-a405-4a0d-a861-caa7563c3f8e> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#Lesson> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/48a27dcd-a405-4a0d-a861-caa7563c3f8e> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "A safety obligation must ride on a field the consumer already reads" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/48a27dcd-a405-4a0d-a861-caa7563c3f8e> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/48a27dcd-a405-4a0d-a861-caa7563c3f8e> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "Documenting an obligation in prose (\"the caller must check X\") is not a mechanism. If nothing in the interface carries X across the boundary, the check does not happen and the failure it was meant to prevent still occurs.\n\nThe instance: HOWL is network-free, so it cannot resolve owl:imports and the caller must supply the complete import closure. A draft addressed this by having HOWL record every observed import into a findings.imports-seen field and stating that the caller was obliged to compare. This failed twice over. The frozen consumer interface (TBoxReport) had no field to carry imports-seen at all, so the value never crossed the boundary; and the CLI — itself a caller — was given no means to resolve or attest imports. Net effect: `howl validate root.ttl` returned exit 0 on an ontology with a dangling import. An unresolved import means missing axioms, missing axioms mean missed incoherence, so this was exactly the silent false pass the whole verdict discipline exists to prevent, arriving through the front door.\n\nThe fix has two parts, and the second is the reusable one. First, make the obligation falsifiable: the input carries an explicit closure ATTESTATION (the set of import IRIs the caller resolved and merged), so HOWL cannot fetch the import but can tell that the caller never claimed to have it. Second, route the consequence through machinery the consumer ALREADY consumes — each unattested import becomes an entry in coverage.omitted, which the consumer's existing \"non-empty ⇒ Inconclusive\" rule already handles. No new field, no new consumer behavior, no remembering.\n\nAccepted cost of the frozen interface: the omission surfaces under a cause label that says out-of-profile where \"unresolved import\" would be more precise. The disposition is right and the label is approximate; that trade is worth taking over a correct label nobody reads.\n\nGeneralizes: when adding a safety property to a system with a frozen interface, ask which existing field the consumer is already obliged to inspect, and make the new condition manifest THERE. A new advisory field is inert by default. Related to [[howl-only-a-complete-run-may-report-coherent]]. Captured 2026-08-12." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/48a27dcd-a405-4a0d-a861-caa7563c3f8e> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/48a27dcd-a405-4a0d-a861-caa7563c3f8e> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTimestamp> "2026-08-13T05:21:37.906535Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/48a27dcd-a405-4a0d-a861-caa7563c3f8e> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTitle> "A safety obligation must ride on a field the consumer already reads" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/515d9810-bbda-48c2-8eac-228feee71f5a> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#Lesson> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/515d9810-bbda-48c2-8eac-228feee71f5a> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Semi-naive rounds must join the delta against a snapshot that includes itself" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/515d9810-bbda-48c2-8eac-228feee71f5a> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/515d9810-bbda-48c2-8eac-228feee71f5a> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "In a barrier-synchronized semi-naive fixpoint, round n must form the frozen snapshot Sₙ = Sₙ₋₁ ∪ Δₙ and join every delta fact against THAT — including Δₙ × Δₙ — while holding generated conclusions for Δₙ₊₁. Joining against the pre-round state Sₙ₋₁ instead silently and permanently loses conclusions.\n\nThe failure: if both premises of a two-premise rule arrive in the same round n, neither sees the other (each scans only Sₙ₋₁, which contains neither), and in round n+1 neither is in the delta any more — so no handler ever fires on that pair. The conclusion is not delayed, it is never derived. Self-joins (a rule joining two facts of the same kind, e.g. HOWL's CR2 over two subsumers of one context and CR7 over two role edges) are most exposed, because same-round co-arrival is their common case rather than a corner.\n\nWhy it slipped through: the pre-round-snapshot rule was introduced for a GOOD reason — keeping a round's own output out of that same round is what makes deterministic barrier semantics work — and the over-restriction looked like the same idea. The two are separable and both are needed: the snapshot must include the round's ARRIVALS (Δₙ) but exclude its OUTPUTS (Δₙ₊₁). Conflating \"don't read your own output\" with \"don't read this round's input\" is the error.\n\nMinor consequence worth knowing when profiling: joining Δₙ against Sₙ ⊇ Δₙ visits each intra-delta pair twice, once from each side. Harmless against a set-valued target, but it is not a bug to be optimized away carelessly.\n\nTest that catches it: an arrival matrix per multi-premise rule covering A-then-B, B-then-A, and A-and-B-in-the-same-round, exercised at more than one worker count. The first two orders catch a missing trigger handler; only the third catches this. Related to [[semi-naive-rules-must-fire-on-every-premise]]. Captured 2026-08-11." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/515d9810-bbda-48c2-8eac-228feee71f5a> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/515d9810-bbda-48c2-8eac-228feee71f5a> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTimestamp> "2026-08-12T01:06:16.361754Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/515d9810-bbda-48c2-8eac-228feee71f5a> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTitle> "Semi-naive rounds must join the delta against a snapshot that includes itself" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/515d9810-bbda-48c2-8eac-228feee71f5a> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#learnedFrom> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/85f90bba-3140-46ac-aa9f-47d76dbd2064> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/67ae1813-6919-47c1-a9a9-ee94731a9c3b> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#Lesson> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/67ae1813-6919-47c1-a9a9-ee94731a9c3b> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Built-in vocabulary needs explicit handling in both directions" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/67ae1813-6919-47c1-a9a9-ee94731a9c3b> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/67ae1813-6919-47c1-a9a9-ee94731a9c3b> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "Specification built-ins fail a naive gate twice, in opposite directions, and the two fixes are independent: they are declared IMPLICITLY (so requiring an explicit declaration is a false fail), and they carry BUILT-IN SEMANTICS (so treating them as ordinary names is a false pass).\n\nThe instance: HOWL's front end required a declaration before an IRI could be tagged class-vs-individual, and otherwise treated any role name as an ordinary role. OWL declares owl:Thing, owl:Nothing, owl:topObjectProperty, owl:bottomObjectProperty and the built-in annotation properties itself, and ontologies neither declare them nor are required to — so the declaration rule rejected essentially every ontology mentioning owl:Thing. Separately, owl:bottomObjectProperty denotes the EMPTY relation, so A ⊑ ∃owl:bottomObjectProperty.⊤ entails A ⊑ ⊥; treated as an ordinary role, CR3 builds an edge and reports A satisfiable — a missed unsatisfiability. owl:topObjectProperty (the universal role) interacts with every existential in the ontology and is not accounted for either.\n\nFixes: pre-populate the input signature with the built-in entities before reading any declaration triple; and gate both special object properties OUT of v0 as out-of-profile rather than implementing partial semantics. Gating rather than implementing follows the standing rule that the cited completeness proof defines the boundary — a role with extra semantics bolted on sits outside the published calculus. Noted in the spec that owl:bottomObjectProperty is tempting to handle with a one-line decode-time rewrite (∃⊥ᵣ.C → ⊥) and that \"handles the common case\" is exactly how a completeness claim degrades into a guess.\n\nGeneralizes: when a language has reserved/built-in vocabulary, enumerate it explicitly and ask BOTH questions of every entry — is it implicitly present (affects acceptance), and does it mean something special (affects semantics). A gate that answers only one gets the other backwards. Related to [[validity-checks-need-false-fail-suites-not-just-false-pass-suites]] and [[a-gate-may-not-be-more-permissive-than-the-proof-it-cites]]. Captured 2026-08-14." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/67ae1813-6919-47c1-a9a9-ee94731a9c3b> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/67ae1813-6919-47c1-a9a9-ee94731a9c3b> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTimestamp> "2026-08-15T01:58:55.163811Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/67ae1813-6919-47c1-a9a9-ee94731a9c3b> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTitle> "Built-in vocabulary needs explicit handling in both directions" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/6a81666b-46a0-4304-9168-19213e65e00d> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#Lesson> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/6a81666b-46a0-4304-9168-19213e65e00d> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Reasoner status is three independent axes, not one status union" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/6a81666b-46a0-4304-9168-19213e65e00d> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/6a81666b-46a0-4304-9168-19213e65e00d> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "Coverage (which axioms were processed), termination (fixpoint / capped / cancelled), and findings (what was derived) are mutually independent. Modelling them as exclusive variants of a single RunStatus union is wrong, and forces the report to lie.\n\nThe gotcha: a run can be BOTH out-of-profile AND resource-limited — the input carried axioms outside the fragment, and the in-profile subset still blew the iteration cap. A union forces a choice between reporting one or the other. This was caught only because the downstream port modelled the two as independent fields (out_of_profile_axioms: Vec<AxiomRef> alongside fixpoint_reached: bool), and the mapping from the union could not be written without losing information. The consumer's data model was the check on ours.\n\nSecond, sharper error from the same collapse: it produced a verdict rule that discarded sound results. Sending every out-of-profile run to \"inconclusive\" is wrong because inference is MONOTONE — restoring an omitted axiom can only add conclusions, never retract one, so a ⊥ derived from a subset of the ontology is a ⊥ in the whole ontology. A finding under partial coverage is DEFINITIVE, not provisional. The correct rule is three-way and asymmetric: any contradiction derived ⇒ incoherent regardless of coverage; no contradiction plus incomplete coverage or non-fixpoint ⇒ inconclusive; no contradiction plus complete coverage and fixpoint ⇒ coherent. The asymmetry is not a compromise, it falls out of monotonicity.\n\nRule of thumb this generalizes to: when a result type has an \"everything went fine\" variant and several failure variants, check whether the failures are actually mutually exclusive before making them a union — and check separately whether positive findings survive each failure mode. Soundness and completeness degrade differently, so a single status tag cannot carry both. Captured 2026-08-11." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/6a81666b-46a0-4304-9168-19213e65e00d> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/6a81666b-46a0-4304-9168-19213e65e00d> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTimestamp> "2026-08-11T15:21:09.231293Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/6a81666b-46a0-4304-9168-19213e65e00d> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTitle> "Reasoner status is three independent axes, not one status union" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/772eff39-94f7-4692-92ea-23f39da47e50> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#Lesson> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/772eff39-94f7-4692-92ea-23f39da47e50> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "The slop verify/test boundary, as of 0.2.1" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/772eff39-94f7-4692-92ea-23f39da47e50> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/772eff39-94f7-4692-92ea-23f39da47e50> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "What `slop verify` and `slop test` can do is decided by how an expression is SHAPED, not by what the claim means. Undocumented upstream; every rule below came from a minimal pair differing in one construct. Status is for slop 0.2.1, re-probed on the upgrade.\n\nSTILL BLOCKED (verified failing on 0.2.1):\n\n1. `or`/`and` IN A BODY MAKE THE RETURN VALUE OPAQUE. Identical `@post` over the same boolean field: `(or (. r b) (> (. r n) 0))` fails; `(if (. r b) true (> (. r n) 0))` verifies. The boolean FIELD is fine; the boolean OPERATOR is the blocker.\n\n2. `@property` OVER A PUSH-BUILT `$result` FAILS, AND POISONS SIBLING `@post`s (#69). It does not go unknown — it fails, and adding it makes a previously-passing `@post` on the same function fail. The reported failure names the `@post`, so the natural diagnosis is that the postcondition is wrong. This is why per-rule faithfulness pairs are still OWED.\n\n3. A LOOP ANYWHERE IN THE BODY makes `$result` opaque — hoist it into a helper and keep the contracted function branch-only.\n\n4. An `if` BETWEEN TWO `record-new`s LOSES FIELD PROJECTION (#70), even when both arms trivially satisfy the claim. Only a single unconditional `record-new` keeps `(. $result f)` visible.\n\n5. TWO EXCLUSIVE BRANCHES THAT EACH PUSH defeat a `<= 1` bound. Set a flag and push ONCE.\n\n6. NO INTERPROCEDURAL REASONING. Where a property must cross a call, add a REDUNDANT local check; where it must cross a loop, `@loop-invariant` carries it.\n\n7. `const` is opaque; `set-elements`/`list-get` are uninterpreted; `(Int 0 ..)` FIELDS are interpreted — which is why an explicit count puts a fixpoint test inside Z3's reach where `set-elements` cannot.\n\nNOW AVAILABLE (0.2.1):\n\n8. `@example` EXECUTES and is the working substitute for (2). Under 0.1.2 an example ran only with scalar-literal arguments; anything else was `SKIP (wildcard args)` and COUNTED AS A PASS (#71, fixed — skips now report as `unrunnable`). Trap: a list-returning example's expected value is SPLAT as literal elements, so it is `(list <elem> …)` with no type argument and `(list)` for empty. `(list Addressed)` — the valid empty-list literal in ordinary CODE — makes the harness treat the type name as an element and fail to compile. An expected value built by a call is likewise splat into its own arguments.\n\n9. Multi-payload union equality (#66) and `set-remove` chain-stranding (#67) are fixed; both had forced defensive shapes that turned out to be independently correct and needed no reverting.\n\nTHE METHOD, which outlives every row: do not diagnose a toolchain limitation from a single failing example, and do not diagnose a FIX from a closed issue. Both directions were wrong here at least once — three \"unverifiable\" verdicts fell to a five-minute probe, and two \"still broken\" assumptions had to be checked rather than inherited. Minimal pairs, every time; and when adding one contract breaks another, suspect interference before either.\n\nMapped 2026-08-19, re-probed against 0.2.1 on 2026-08-21. 23 verified contracts, 0 failing; 6 executing examples." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/772eff39-94f7-4692-92ea-23f39da47e50> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/772eff39-94f7-4692-92ea-23f39da47e50> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasRationale> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/Rationale/1902593f-83e2-4983-a722-a7fff009f112> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/772eff39-94f7-4692-92ea-23f39da47e50> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTimestamp> "2026-08-22T02:34:26.763729Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/772eff39-94f7-4692-92ea-23f39da47e50> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTitle> "The slop verify/test boundary, as of 0.2.1" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/772eff39-94f7-4692-92ea-23f39da47e50> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#learnedFrom> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/85e6f9fb-40ea-497b-b689-3074f1a4ad52> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/772eff39-94f7-4692-92ea-23f39da47e50> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#supersedes> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/351e4c85-8221-400b-ad97-ffc72f8d83ee> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/9443f5d9-0945-4fb6-aea0-6ef477869ece> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#Lesson> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/9443f5d9-0945-4fb6-aea0-6ef477869ece> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Seed the delta, not the store — semi-naive bootstrap" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/9443f5d9-0945-4fb6-aea0-6ef477869ece> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/9443f5d9-0945-4fb6-aea0-6ef477869ece> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "In a semi-naive fixpoint, initial facts must be placed in the INITIAL DELTA, not written directly into the fact store. Writing them into the store leaves every work queue empty, so the driver reaches its termination condition before firing a single rule.\n\nThe instance: HOWL initializes S(N) = {N, ⊤} for every context. A draft specified this as seeding the `subsumers` set. Since rules fire only on facts arriving in Δₙ, and `active` tracks contexts with non-empty queues, `active` would be empty at round 0 — the driver reports `fixpoint`, and CR1 through CR3 never process N or ⊤ at all. Correct form is S₋₁ := ∅ with Δ₀(N) := { derived-sub(N), derived-sub(⊤) }, so round 0 forms S₀ = ∅ ∪ Δ₀ and joins against it, with every context starting active.\n\nWhy this one is especially dangerous rather than merely wrong: the failure is not a crash or a missing conclusion in an edge case. The run terminates instantly, reports `fixpoint` with empty coverage omissions — i.e. COMPLETE — and returns an empty classification. Under the verdict rule that is a clean \"coherent\" verdict. Sound, catastrophically incomplete, and indistinguishable from success at every interface.\n\nIt is also nearly untestable by accident. Any fixture with ordinary axioms generates deltas through other paths and masks the bug. The test that catches it must have the initial seed as its ONLY enabling premise — e.g. `⊤ ⊑ C` over a single otherwise-axiom-free class, where the answer depends entirely on ⊤ ∈ Δ₀ reaching CR1.\n\nGeneralizes to any delta-driven fixpoint including GROWL's: whenever a design says \"the state is initialized to X\", ask whether X is visible to the rules, which for semi-naive evaluation means asking whether X is in a delta. Related to [[semi-naive-rounds-must-join-the-delta-against-a-snapshot-that-includes-itself]]. Captured 2026-08-12." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/9443f5d9-0945-4fb6-aea0-6ef477869ece> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "superseded" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/9443f5d9-0945-4fb6-aea0-6ef477869ece> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTimestamp> "2026-08-13T05:40:38.239105Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/9443f5d9-0945-4fb6-aea0-6ef477869ece> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTitle> "Seed the delta, not the store — semi-naive bootstrap" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/9443f5d9-0945-4fb6-aea0-6ef477869ece> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#isSupersededBy> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/efa41206-ca53-4fa3-80f9-505ac464bddc> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/9443f5d9-0945-4fb6-aea0-6ef477869ece> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#learnedFrom> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/5cecf538-1be2-4f57-a979-888812c3b7d4> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/9443f5d9-0945-4fb6-aea0-6ef477869ece> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#learnedFrom> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/85f90bba-3140-46ac-aa9f-47d76dbd2064> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/977a6eb2-028a-405d-b048-63ae24fb3ad2> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#Lesson> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/977a6eb2-028a-405d-b048-63ae24fb3ad2> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Check what the generator emits before keying a Set on a type" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/977a6eb2-028a-405d-b048-63ae24fb3ad2> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/977a6eb2-028a-405d-b048-63ae24fb3ad2> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "Before using a type as a `(Set T)` element or `(Map T _)` key when its equality is GENERATED rather than written, read the emitted code. Generated structural equality that silently projects onto one field is worse than no equality, because it yields a plausible wrong answer instead of an error.\n\nTHE INSTANCE, now historical. Through slop 0.1.2 the transpiler registered only a union variant's FIRST payload type for hash/eq generation and emitted no multi-field branch, so for `(derived-succ RoleId Node)` it produced a comparison of the RoleId that never examined the Node. `(derived-succ r Y₁)` and `(derived-succ r Y₂)` were EQUAL to a `(Set Derived)`. For a reasoner that is severe: the novelty test `Δₙ₊₁ := conclusions \\\\ Sₙ` is exactly a set-membership check over derived facts, so every role filler after the first would have been swallowed — under-derivation with no error, worst on ontologies with many fillers on one role, and invisible to any single-filler fixture. Fixed in slop 0.2.1 (#66) and confirmed by direct test.\n\nWHY IT WAS FOUND AT ALL, which is the transferable part: by reading the transpiler's emission path before writing code that depended on it, not by testing. No test would have existed, because nobody writes a test for a case they do not suspect. The general move is to treat generated equality, hashing and ordering as unverified until you have seen the emitted form — they are the operations most likely to be silently partial, and the ones whose failures look like ordinary results.\n\nSECOND-ORDER LESSON, from the fix landing: a defensive shape that is ALSO the right shape costs nothing when the defect is repaired. HOWL keyed its round delta on single-payload unions to avoid this bug; on upgrading to 0.2.1 nothing had to be reverted, because that shape is independently what the commit-at-barrier driver wants. A workaround that distorts the design has to be paid back; one that coincides with the design does not. Prefer the latter when both are available.\n\nFound 2026-08-19, fixed upstream and re-verified 2026-08-21." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/977a6eb2-028a-405d-b048-63ae24fb3ad2> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/977a6eb2-028a-405d-b048-63ae24fb3ad2> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasRationale> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/Rationale/2126a382-6db4-4ba9-b98e-86fc8f963903> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/977a6eb2-028a-405d-b048-63ae24fb3ad2> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTimestamp> "2026-08-22T02:33:31.989685Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/977a6eb2-028a-405d-b048-63ae24fb3ad2> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTitle> "Check what the generator emits before keying a Set on a type" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/977a6eb2-028a-405d-b048-63ae24fb3ad2> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#learnedFrom> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/f15852f8-c7e1-4d93-a347-d915de5e041e> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/977a6eb2-028a-405d-b048-63ae24fb3ad2> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#supersedes> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/0165b8ad-6735-4c1b-b8dc-970c9bc1b612> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/a252a1c1-6396-4e70-af3a-9485d71dcb4e> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#Lesson> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/a252a1c1-6396-4e70-af3a-9485d71dcb4e> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Set difference breaks the partial-results-are-sound guarantee" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/a252a1c1-6396-4e70-af3a-9485d71dcb4e> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/a252a1c1-6396-4e70-af3a-9485d71dcb4e> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "A reasoner's usual guarantee — partial results are sound, only their silences are uninformative — does NOT survive set difference. Subtraction is non-monotone in its second argument, so a silence in the subtrahend becomes a positive claim in the output.\n\nThe instance: HOWL serves the port's subsumption_delta as classify(candidate) − classify(baseline). If the baseline run was incomplete, a subsumption entailed by BOTH theories can be missing from the baseline result, and the difference then reports it as NEW — an assertion about the candidate that is simply false. Every other partial result in the system is a sound lower bound; this one manufactures false positives, so it cannot inherit the general verdict rule and needs its own precondition.\n\nRule adopted: baseline completeness is a PRECONDITION of the diff, not a caveat on it. If the baseline run has non-empty omitted coverage or did not reach fixpoint, suppress the delta entirely rather than emitting a partial one — an empty delta under non-empty combined coverage reads as inconclusive through the existing machinery, whereas a partial delta looks like an answer. Combined coverage is the union of both runs' omissions; combined fixpoint is the conjunction.\n\nGeneralizes: whenever a derived output is computed by subtracting, complementing, or otherwise negating one incomplete result against another, re-derive the soundness argument from scratch. Monotone accumulation preserves soundness under truncation; anything with a negation in it does not. Look for this wherever a \"delta\", \"new since\", \"removed\", or \"missing\" view is built over results that can be partial. Captured 2026-08-12." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/a252a1c1-6396-4e70-af3a-9485d71dcb4e> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/a252a1c1-6396-4e70-af3a-9485d71dcb4e> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTimestamp> "2026-08-13T05:58:04.641197Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/a252a1c1-6396-4e70-af3a-9485d71dcb4e> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTitle> "Set difference breaks the partial-results-are-sound guarantee" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/a2a6b77a-4f5d-4afe-81e7-3a7af46af305> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#Lesson> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/a2a6b77a-4f5d-4afe-81e7-3a7af46af305> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "A green test run proves nothing until you have seen it go red" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/a2a6b77a-4f5d-4afe-81e7-3a7af46af305> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/a2a6b77a-4f5d-4afe-81e7-3a7af46af305> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "Verify that a test harness EXECUTES before trusting what it reports. A runner that counts skipped tests as passed produces a suite that certifies nothing while looking green, and nothing in the output distinguishes it from one that works.\n\nTHE INSTANCE. Six `@example` blocks were added to HOWL's completion rules, and `slop test` reported `6 passed, 0 failed`. Every one had actually been `SKIP (wildcard args)`: through slop 0.1.2 an example executed only when every argument was a scalar literal, and any fixture call — which every rule fixture must be, since a Context cannot be written as a literal — was skipped and then folded into the pass count. GROWL was in the same position with 47 examples, none of which had ever run.\n\nThe tell was noticing the word SKIP in verbose output that a summary line had already declared green. Nothing else would have surfaced it: the count was right, the exit status was zero, and CI would have stayed green forever.\n\nTHE PRACTICE. For any new test mechanism, before relying on it: (1) write an assertion that MUST fail and confirm it does; (2) read the per-test output, not just the summary; (3) mutate the code under test and confirm the specific test goes red. Step 3 is the one that generalises — it is the same discipline as mutation-testing a contract, applied to the harness rather than to the code. HOWL's examples were mutation-tested this way once they ran: removing CR2's second self-join arm turns exactly one example red, which is what makes the other five meaningful.\n\nWHY THIS CLASS OF BUG IS WORSE THAN A BROKEN TEST. A test that errors gets fixed. A test that silently does not run accumulates: it is added in good faith to pin a regression, it reports success, and the regression it was written for lands anyway. The defect is in the layer everything else is measured against, so every downstream conclusion inherits it — the same reason a reasoner must not report `coherent` for a run it could not complete.\n\nReported upstream as slop-lang/slop#71 and fixed in 0.2.1. The requested fix was deliberately the cheap one — count skips separately and never fold them into `passed` — because it is worth doing even if the underlying limitation is never lifted. `unrunnable` now appears as its own column.\n\nFound 2026-08-20; fix verified 2026-08-21." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/a2a6b77a-4f5d-4afe-81e7-3a7af46af305> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/a2a6b77a-4f5d-4afe-81e7-3a7af46af305> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTimestamp> "2026-08-22T02:33:52.144194Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/a2a6b77a-4f5d-4afe-81e7-3a7af46af305> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTitle> "A green test run proves nothing until you have seen it go red" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/a2a6b77a-4f5d-4afe-81e7-3a7af46af305> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#learnedFrom> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/85e6f9fb-40ea-497b-b689-3074f1a4ad52> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/afc6118d-5264-44db-97f1-907fea8427ad> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#Lesson> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/afc6118d-5264-44db-97f1-907fea8427ad> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Decode before gating: a gate needs the AST, not the syntax" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/afc6118d-5264-44db-97f1-907fea8427ad> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/afc6118d-5264-44db-97f1-907fea8427ad> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "A profile gate that must compare structured values has to run over decoded abstract syntax, not over the concrete serialization. The unlock is that DECODING IS NOT ACCEPTING — an unsupported variant decodes into an AST node the gate then rejects, so decoding first costs no permissiveness.\n\nThe instance: HOWL's range/composition condition requires \"the same class expression\" imposed on two roles. A draft ran all gates before the RDF→structure pass, leaving only triples available. Two structurally identical range expressions can use different blank nodes, so comparing RDF node identity would reject a valid ontology, while decoding expressions inside the gate would duplicate the structural mapper. Neither is acceptable; the fix is ordering.\n\nWhat made the wrong order look necessary: a separate and genuine constraint said the RBox gates must see property chains BEFORE binary decomposition, because decomposition introduces fresh intermediate roles that can hide a non-regular dependency cycle. \"Before decomposition\" got conflated with \"before the structural pass\" — they are different boundaries, and the front end has room for both. Correct order: header/import reconciliation → decode to AST (and build the input signature) → gate over the AST, chains as written → normalize accepted axioms → initialize.\n\nSecond bug the same reordering fixed: the context registry was keyed on nodes occurring in normalized axioms, so a class that is declared and otherwise unmentioned (`:C a owl:Class .`) received no context at all — the reasoner could not answer C ⊑ ⊤, list C in a hierarchy, or serve a probe naming it. Not an error, just silent absence. Keying the registry on the INPUT SIGNATURE (every IRI appearing in a declaration or a structural position, gathered during decoding) fixes it, and gives \"occurs in the input\" a single precise meaning used by the unsatisfiable-class definition and the output projection too.\n\nGeneral rule: when ordering front-end passes, list the constraints each pass imposes on the others explicitly rather than reasoning pairwise — the two constraints here were individually correct and jointly unsatisfiable in the order chosen. Captured 2026-08-12." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/afc6118d-5264-44db-97f1-907fea8427ad> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/afc6118d-5264-44db-97f1-907fea8427ad> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTimestamp> "2026-08-13T06:10:45.770222Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/afc6118d-5264-44db-97f1-907fea8427ad> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTitle> "Decode before gating: a gate needs the AST, not the syntax" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/bb5a926e-bd43-4a54-8e86-bac9dbfd630f> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#Lesson> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/bb5a926e-bd43-4a54-8e86-bac9dbfd630f> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "The Z3 contract boundary in SLOP is or/and in the body" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/bb5a926e-bd43-4a54-8e86-bac9dbfd630f> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/bb5a926e-bd43-4a54-8e86-bac9dbfd630f> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "What `slop verify` can discharge is decided by how an expression is SHAPED, not by what the claim means, and the boundary is documented nowhere. Mapped by controlled probe on HOWL's scaffold.\n\nTHE HEADLINE: `or` AND `and` IN A FUNCTION BODY MAKE THE RETURN VALUE OPAQUE. Two functions carrying an identical `@post` over the same boolean record field differ only in body — `(or (. r b) (> (. r n) 0))` fails with a counterexample, `(if (. r b) true (> (. r n) 0))` verifies. Boolean record FIELDS are perfectly visible; boolean OPERATORS are not. Reach for `if` before concluding a claim is unverifiable. This one substitution, semantics unchanged, unblocked three contracts.\n\nWHAT TRANSLATES AND PROVES\n- Record fields, including Bool, and `list-len`, with nested access like `(. (. o coverage) omitted)`.\n- `(@post (implies $result {braced-comparison}))` — bare `$result` as ANTECEDENT.\n- `(@post (implies {braced} {$result == X}))`, including enum results and `!=`.\n- `(@post {$result == (comparison)})`, and `match` inside a `@post`.\n- `@property` with `implies`, but ONLY unbraced — the same thing with `{...}` around operands fails to translate.\n- `while` loops, with or without `@loop-invariant`.\n\nWHAT DOES NOT\n- `or`/`and` in a body, as above.\n- `const` values: `{$result == EXIT_ERROR}` fails on an out-of-range counterexample; the literal `3` verifies.\n- `set-elements` and `list-get` — uninterpreted functions.\n- NO INTERPROCEDURAL REASONING. A callee is uninterpreted; the counterexample says so literally (`fn_outcome-is-complete_1=[else -> True]`).\n\nTHREE MOVES THAT TURN AN UNPROVABLE CLAIM INTO A PROVED ONE\n1. Rewrite `or`/`and` as `if`. Same semantics, verifiable instead of not.\n2. Add a REDUNDANT LOCAL CHECK where a safety property would otherwise depend on a callee. HOWL's `verdict` delegated its coverage test, so \"a coverage gap cannot yield coherent\" was unprovable; an explicit local early-out — redundant for the ANSWER, load-bearing for the PROOF — made it structural. Worth it for a genuine safety property, not as a habit.\n3. Use `@loop-invariant` to carry a fact across a loop whose calls are opaque. HOWL's `saturate` proves `iteration <= max-iterations` this way despite both calls in the loop being uninterpreted.\n\nAND PREFER A TYPE OVER A CONTRACT WHERE THE SHAPE ALLOWS. `emit-edge` returns an `EdgePair` record rather than a two-element list, so \"both halves exist\" is true by construction and the destinations are provable field accesses. As a list it needed `list-get` — unprovable, and only testable.\n\nDISCIPLINE. Mark genuinely undischargeable obligations OWED in-source with the reason and cover them by test; do not state a postcondition the prover cannot discharge, which reads as coverage while proving nothing, and do not state vacuous ones like `(list-len $result) >= 0`. MUTATION-TEST the contracts — removing the verdict coverage check, inverting a `@property`, and dropping an arm of `findings-are-incoherent` were each confirmed to make `slop verify` fail. Without that there is no evidence a contract constrains anything.\n\nMETHOD NOTE, the reason this record was superseded once already: do not diagnose a prover limitation from one failing example. Isolate the variable. Three separate \"not reasonably verifiable\" verdicts here were all wrong, and each was reachable by changing the shape of the code rather than weakening the claim." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/bb5a926e-bd43-4a54-8e86-bac9dbfd630f> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "superseded" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/bb5a926e-bd43-4a54-8e86-bac9dbfd630f> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasRationale> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/Rationale/6c1b63cb-e98b-4139-bb7d-32e9560c3f08> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/bb5a926e-bd43-4a54-8e86-bac9dbfd630f> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTimestamp> "2026-08-19T18:00:41.744248Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/bb5a926e-bd43-4a54-8e86-bac9dbfd630f> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTitle> "The Z3 contract boundary in SLOP is or/and in the body" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/bb5a926e-bd43-4a54-8e86-bac9dbfd630f> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#isSupersededBy> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/351e4c85-8221-400b-ad97-ffc72f8d83ee> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/bb5a926e-bd43-4a54-8e86-bac9dbfd630f> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#learnedFrom> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/85e6f9fb-40ea-497b-b689-3074f1a4ad52> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/bb5a926e-bd43-4a54-8e86-bac9dbfd630f> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#supersedes> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/bbd185cd-89f4-44c6-a362-fd423e39947d> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/bbd185cd-89f4-44c6-a362-fd423e39947d> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#Lesson> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/bbd185cd-89f4-44c6-a362-fd423e39947d> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "The Z3 contract boundary in SLOP is about expression shape" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/bbd185cd-89f4-44c6-a362-fd423e39947d> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/bbd185cd-89f4-44c6-a362-fd423e39947d> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "What `slop verify` can discharge is decided less by what a claim MEANS than by how the expression is SHAPED, and the boundary is not documented anywhere. Mapped empirically on HOWL's scaffold (2026-08-17), taking verified contracts from 6 to 11.\n\nWHAT TRANSLATES AND PROVES\n- Integer record fields and `list-len`, including nested access like `(. (. o coverage) omitted)`.\n- `(@post (implies $result {braced-comparison}))` — bare `$result` as the ANTECEDENT.\n- `(@post (implies {braced-comparison} {$result == X}))` — including enum-valued results (`{$result == 'profile-el}`) and `!=`.\n- `(@post {$result == (comparison)})`.\n- `match` inside a `@post`, with braced comparisons in the arms.\n- `@property` with `implies` — but ONLY unbraced. `(@property p (implies (== ... 0) (not $result)))` verifies; the same thing with `{...}` around the operands fails to translate.\n\nWHAT DOES NOT\n- BOOL RECORD FIELDS are opaque. `(. f inconsistent)` yields a counterexample every time, while the integer-valued `(list-len (. f unsatisfiable))` on the same record proves fine.\n- `const` values are opaque: `{$result == EXIT_ERROR}` fails with a counterexample of an out-of-range integer; the same postcondition with literal `3` verifies.\n- `set-elements` and `list-get` are uninterpreted functions, so set emptiness and positional list contents are unreachable.\n- NO INTERPROCEDURAL REASONING. A callee is modelled as an uninterpreted function; the counterexample literally shows `fn_outcome-is-complete_1=[else -> True]`. Any property depending on what a helper returns is unprovable.\n\nTHE TWO NON-OBVIOUS CONSEQUENCES, both of which changed the code:\n\n1. ONE OPAQUE OPERAND POISONS THE WHOLE EXPRESSION. `(or (. f inconsistent) (> (list-len ...) 0))` is untranslatable, so even the arm that is purely about an integer cannot be discharged. Rewriting as `(if (> (list-len ...) 0) true (. f inconsistent))` — semantically identical — makes the reachable branch syntactically explicit and the contract verifies.\n\n2. A REDUNDANT LOCAL CHECK CAN BUY A PROOF. HOWL's verdict function delegated its coverage test to `outcome-is-complete`, and the safety property \"a coverage gap cannot yield coherent\" was therefore unprovable. Re-adding an explicit local coverage early-out — redundant for the ANSWER, load-bearing for the PROOF — turned it into a structural fact about one function. Worth doing for a genuine safety property; not worth doing everywhere.\n\nDISCIPLINE THAT FOLLOWS. Mark undischargeable obligations `OWED` in-source with the reason and cover them by test, rather than either omitting them silently or stating a postcondition the prover cannot discharge — the latter reads as coverage in the summary while proving nothing. Avoid vacuous contracts for the same reason: `(list-len $result) >= 0` is true of every possible implementation.\n\nAnd mutation-test the contracts. Removing the verdict coverage check, inverting a `@property`, and dropping an arm of `findings-are-incoherent` were each confirmed to make `slop verify` fail — otherwise there is no evidence a contract constrains anything." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/bbd185cd-89f4-44c6-a362-fd423e39947d> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "superseded" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Lesson/bbd185cd-89f4-44c6-a362-fd423e39947d> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTimestamp> "2026-08-18T02:25:11.661288Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
